WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.165
Authentication factors are categories of proof.

2
00:00:03.165 --> 00:00:07.084
Something you know includes passwords or memorized secrets.

3
00:00:07.084 --> 00:00:15.751
Something you have includes a hardware security key, authenticator app, certificate, registered device, or platform authenticator.

4
00:00:15.751 --> 00:00:19.218
Something you are refers to biometrics at a high level.

5
00:00:19.218 --> 00:00:29.619
Some systems also consider something you do or somewhere you are as contextual signals, although those are often better treated as risk signals than primary proof.

6
00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:33.839
An authenticator is the concrete mechanism used to present proof.

7
00:00:33.839 --> 00:00:45.144
A password, one-time code, push approval, hardware-backed key, certificate, biometric unlock of a local device credential, or passkey can all participate in authentication.

8
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:57.504
The strength of the authenticator depends on implementation, user experience, recovery processes, replay resistance, device binding, and whether it resists phishing or social engineering.

9
00:00:57.504 --> 00:01:01.498
Not every second factor provides the same level of protection.

10
00:01:01.498 --> 00:01:09.563
One-time codes can reduce simple password-only risk, but reusable codes may still be captured and replayed in some scenarios.

11
00:01:09.563 --> 00:01:16.044
Push approvals are convenient, but unclear prompts or repeated prompts can create approval fatigue.

12
00:01:16.044 --> 00:01:28.781
Hardware security keys, platform authenticators, and passkeys can provide stronger phishing resistance when they bind authentication to the legitimate service and avoid reusable shared secrets.

13
00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:32.926
Matching authenticator strength to risk is the practical skill.

14
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:43.854
A low-risk internal tool may not need the same assurance as privileged administration, financial approval, production deployment, or access to sensitive personal data.

15
00:01:43.854 --> 00:01:55.686
Strong authentication programs use the right factors for the context, protect enrollment and recovery, and recognize that usability and supportability are part of security outcomes.
